---
/**
 * NavDropdown.astro — ONE dropdown, used by every dropdown in the header.
 *
 * WHY IT IS A COMPONENT AND NOT A SECOND COPY. Until 2026-08-04 the Products
 * mega-menu was written inline in Nav.astro against hardcoded element ids
 * (`nav-products-trigger`, `nav-mega-panel`) and driven by a script that did
 * `getElementById` on both. It worked, and it worked for exactly one menu. The
 * owner's decision to add a Resources menu had two possible shapes: paste the
 * block and its handlers a second time, or make the block take an argument.
 * Pasting is how this codebase acquired the fork pattern it has been unpicking
 * all week — 26 of 43 page headings without a gradient, section eyebrows in two
 * flavours, a nav injected by a script and styled by a stylesheet nothing else
 * used. A second menu written a second time is that defect being created live,
 * with the fix for the last one still on screen.
 *
 * So the extraction IS the feature. There is one trigger, one panel, one set of
 * key bindings and one pointer behaviour, and a third menu is one entry in
 * src/data/nav.ts.
 *
 * ── THE SCRIPT RUNS ONCE, NOT ONCE PER INSTANCE ─────────────────────────────
 *
 * The initialiser is booted ONCE per document — from src/scripts/shell.ts, via
 * layouts/Base.astro — however many times this component is used, so a second
 * menu costs no second copy of anything. It therefore has to be written for N
 * menus rather than for `this` one: it selects every `.ca-dropdown` on the page
 * and initialises each. The `data-js` flag it stamps is also the guard against
 * double-binding.
 *
 * That was true when the code was a hoisted <script> at the foot of this file
 * too — Astro emitted it once per page — but it was ALSO being written into all
 * 44 documents rather than fetched as a file, which is why it moved on
 * 2026-08-04. See the note where it used to be, and ADR 0010.
 *
 * ── ROLES: THIS IS A DISCLOSURE, NOT AN ARIA MENU ───────────────────────────
 *
 * The previous markup carried role="menu" on the panel, role="menuitem" on each
 * link and aria-haspopup="true" on the trigger, and that combination was wrong
 * in a way that made the menu HARDER to use with a screen reader, not easier.
 * role="menu" is the application-menu pattern: it promises a single tab stop
 * with roving tabindex and arrow-key traversal, and it makes a screen reader
 * announce "menu, 7 items" and switch to application mode. The links inside
 * were ordinary tabbable anchors with no roving tabindex and no arrow handling,
 * so the promise was not kept — the reader was told to press arrow keys by a
 * control that only answered Tab.
 *
 * The APG pattern for a navigation menu made of links is Disclosure Navigation:
 * a button with aria-expanded and aria-controls, and a plain container of
 * links. That is what this is now. Tab moves through the links, because they
 * are links. Arrow keys ALSO work, because the owner asked for them and the
 * disclosure pattern permits them as an enhancement — the difference from
 * role="menu" is that they are a bonus rather than the only way in.
 *
 * This is a deliberate departure from what shipped, taken on the standing rule
 * that "as per architecture and design" means best practice rather than
 * whatever was recorded as agreed. The prior roles are evidence, not authority.
 *
 * ── IT MORPHS BETWEEN MENUS. A-95, 2026-08-05 ───────────────────────────────
 *
 * The panel used to appear and disappear. A reader moving from one trigger to
 * the next now sees the incoming panel take the outgoing one's box and grow or
 * shrink to its own, so two menus read as one surface changing shape.
 *
 * IT CHANGES NOTHING ABOVE. The morph is a size, written as an inline width and
 * height on a panel that is already `position: absolute` — out of flow, so it
 * cannot move a pixel of the page behind it — and removed again when the travel
 * lands. It touches no role, no aria-expanded, no close path and no focus, and
 * it is inert under prefers-reduced-motion. The disclosure argued above is the
 * disclosure that ships.
 *
 * WHERE IT IS ARGUED: the two transitioned properties and the clip in the
 * <style> block below, the mechanism in src/scripts/nav-dropdown.ts. Both carry
 * the measurements the design was settled on.
 *
 * ── WHAT IS CARRIED FORWARD UNCHANGED ───────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Every close path that tests/nav-dropdown.spec.js was written to protect:
 * JavaScript owns the open state alone (the CSS-only :hover/:focus-within
 * fallback is retired the moment `data-js` is stamped, which is what made the
 * old panel unclosable); the pointer affordance is gated to `(hover: hover) and
 * (pointer: fine)` so a tap on a touch device is not cancelled by its own
 * synthetic mouseenter; the close is delayed so diagonal travel from trigger to
 * panel does not dismiss it, and it yields to the keyboard.
 */
import type { NavMenu } from '../../data/nav';

interface Props {
  menu: NavMenu;
  /** True when the current route sits under one of the menu's activeRoutes. */
  active: boolean;
}

const { menu, active } = Astro.props;

const triggerId = `nav-${menu.id}-trigger`;
const panelId = `nav-${menu.id}-panel`;

/**
 * Icon accents come from tokens, never from a literal. `mark` is the violet
 * that marks the primary row in a column; `teal` is the supporting rows.
 */
const ACCENT: Record<'mark' | 'teal', string> = {
  mark: 'var(--c-ambient-end)',
  teal: 'var(--c-ambient-start)',
};

/**
 * The panel is a grid of as many columns as the menu declares. It was a
 * hardcoded `repeat(2, ...)` with a 560px floor, which was correct for exactly
 * the one two-column menu that existed and would have been wrong for both menus
 * the moment Resources arrived.
 */
const columnCount = menu.columns.length;
---

<div class="ca-dropdown" data-nav-dropdown>
  <button
    type="button"
    class="ca-dropdown-trigger"
    id={triggerId}
    aria-expanded="false"
    aria-controls={panelId}
    data-active={active ? 'true' : undefined}
    aria-current={active ? 'page' : undefined}
  >
    {menu.label}
    <svg class="ca-chevron" width="10" height="6" viewBox="0 0 10 6" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
      <path d="M1 1l4 4 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
    </svg>
  </button>

  <div
    class="ca-mega"
    id={panelId}
    aria-labelledby={triggerId}
    style={`--mega-cols:${columnCount}`}
  >
    {
      menu.columns.map((col) => (
        <div class="ca-mega-col">
          <span class="grouplabel ca-mega-label">{col.label}</span>
          {col.items.map((item) => (
            <a href={item.href} class="ca-mega-item">
              <span class="ca-mega-icon" style={`color:${ACCENT[item.accent]}`} aria-hidden="true">
                {item.icon === 'dot' && (
                  <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">
                    <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="5" />
                  </svg>
                )}
                {item.icon === 'grid' && (
                  <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                    <rect x="3" y="3" width="7" height="7" />
                    <rect x="14" y="3" width="7" height="7" />
                    <rect x="14" y="14" width="7" height="7" />
                    <rect x="3" y="14" width="7" height="7" />
                  </svg>
                )}
                {item.icon === 'arrow' && (
                  <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                    <line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12" />
                    <polyline points="13 6 19 12 13 18" />
                  </svg>
                )}
                {item.icon === 'book' && (
                  <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                    <path d="M4 4.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2H20v18H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 0 4 22z" />
                    <path d="M4 17.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 15H20" />
                  </svg>
                )}
              </span>
              <span>
                <strong>{item.label}</strong>
                <span class="ca-mega-desc">{item.description}</span>
              </span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</div>

{/*
  THE INITIALISER IS IN src/scripts/nav-dropdown.ts.
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Unchanged, and still written for N menus rather than for one — the reasoning
  in the frontmatter above is all still the reasoning, and it stays here with
  the markup it describes. Only the code moved, on 2026-08-04, under ADR 0010.

  WHY. A hoisted component <script> small enough to fit Astro's inline limit is
  written INTO the document rather than emitted as a file, so this one shipped
  identically in all 44 documents at 1,601 B a copy. It is booted once now from
  src/scripts/shell.ts, which layouts/Base.astro carries as the site's single
  client entry.

  The sentence in the frontmatter that says "Astro hoists and bundles a
  component's <script> and emits it once per page" is still true and is still
  the reason this is written for N menus: one boot, however many dropdowns.
*/}

<style>
  .ca-dropdown {
    position: relative;
  }

  /* Colour is NOT declared here. Every interactive colour in the header is
     stated once, in Nav.astro — see the comment on the nav ink block there.
     A trigger that painted itself would be the second half of the two-hover-
     colours defect that block was written to end. */
  .ca-dropdown-trigger {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* O-48. Same rule, same reason as `.ca-nav-links > a` in Nav.astro: the
       WCAG 2.2 target is stated as a min-block-size rather than bought with
       padding, because --t-body is fluid and a padding figure that clears 44px
       at one viewport falls under it at another. This element is already
       inline-flex and centred, so the height is the only missing half. */
    min-block-size: 44px;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    font-weight: 600;
    background: none;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 0.4rem 0;
  }
  /* Longhands, never the `transition` shorthand: the shorthand also resets
     transition-delay and transition-behavior, which is the reason motion.css
     lists its properties by name too.

     No prefers-reduced-motion block is needed and adding one would be a second
     owner for the same decision. tokens.css collapses --dur-fast to 0.01ms
     under `reduce`, so the chevron arrives at its rotated state instantly
     rather than travelling — which is the site's stated rule, because a
     state readout that never arrives is worse than one that does not animate. */
  .ca-chevron {
    transition-property: transform;
    transition-duration: var(--dur-fast);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease);
  }
  .ca-dropdown-trigger[aria-expanded='true'] .ca-chevron {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  .ca-mega {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(100% + 0.75rem);
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    /* As many columns as the menu declares, set inline from the data. It was
       `repeat(2, minmax(260px, 1fr))` with a 560px floor, which was right for
       the single two-column menu that existed and wrong for both of the
       one-column menus that replaced it. */
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--mega-cols, 1), minmax(320px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
    /* ── THE MORPH, CSS HALF. A-95, 2026-08-05 ─────────────────────────────
     *
     * Two properties and a clip. src/scripts/nav-dropdown.ts measures the
     * panel a reader is travelling FROM, writes that box here as an inline
     * width and height, and immediately writes this panel's own; these three
     * declarations are what turn the second write into a travel instead of a
     * jump. The full argument — what is measured, why the "from" box is read
     * live rather than remembered, why the keyboard path has nothing to morph
     * — is beside the code that does it.
     *
     * LONGHANDS, NEVER THE SHORTHAND, for the same reason the chevron above
     * gives: the shorthand also resets transition-delay and
     * transition-behavior, which is why motion.css lists its properties by
     * name too.
     *
     * NO prefers-reduced-motion BLOCK HERE, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
     * tokens.css collapses --dur-fast to 0.01ms under `reduce`, so the panel
     * arrives at its size instead of travelling to it — the site's stated
     * rule, and the same argument the chevron makes fifteen lines up. The
     * script ALSO reads the preference and skips the measurement entirely, so
     * under `reduce` no inline size is ever written and there is nothing for
     * these declarations to act on. Two owners for one decision would be the
     * defect; this is one decision honoured in the one place that can make
     * the behaviour genuinely inert, backed by the token that makes the
     * declaration harmless if it is ever reached.
     *
     * --dur-fast RATHER THAN --dur, and the number is not arbitrary: 0.18s
     * against the 160ms delayed close that hands the panels over. The
     * outgoing panel therefore leaves as the incoming one settles, instead of
     * vanishing while it is still growing.
     *
     * THE CLIP IS LOAD-BEARING. A grid row keeps its content height and
     * overflows a box shorter than itself, so for the 92px the taller menu
     * spends growing into its own height its last row would otherwise be
     * drawn outside the panel's border and its rounded corners. Nothing is
     * clipped at rest — measured 320.31 and 412.52 against content that fits
     * both — and the 20px padding keeps every focus ring inside the box. */
    overflow: hidden;
    transition-property: width, height;
    transition-duration: var(--dur-fast);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease);
    background: var(--c-panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-panel);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-panel);
    padding: 1.25rem;
    /* ── FROSTED, 2026-08-03 ───────────────────────────────────────────────
     *
     * Owner instruction: *"Glassmorphism & Frosted Surfaces. Increase usage
     * only where it enhances depth."* This panel qualifies on the only test
     * that matters: it floats over the page, so there is something behind it
     * to refract.
     *
     * THE TRANSLUCENCY IS THE CHANGE, NOT THE FILTER, and that distinction is
     * the whole lesson from the "where is glassmorphism?" round on 2026-08-03.
     * `backdrop-filter` was already computing on every card on the site —
     * measured, 21 of 21 on /pricing — and drawing nothing, because a backdrop
     * filter blurs what it is OVER and blurring a flat fill produces a flat
     * fill. --c-panel is opaque, so adding a filter here alone would have been
     * the same non-change a second time. It needs both.
     *
     * 82%, AND THE NAV IS THE PRECEDENT. `.ca-nav` already runs
     * rgba(5, 7, 14, 0.72) with --blur-glass over live content and passes
     * contrast, so this is the shipped recipe at a slightly higher opacity
     * rather than a new one. Higher because a nav bar holds four short labels
     * and this holds product names with descriptions under them.
     *
     * @supports KEEPS THE OPAQUE PANEL. Where there is no backdrop-filter the
     * declaration above stands unmodified and the menu is the solid panel it
     * has always been. A translucent panel WITHOUT a blur behind it is the one
     * genuinely bad outcome here: page text showing straight through a menu. */
  }

  @supports ((backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(1px))) {
    .ca-mega {
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-panel) 82%, transparent);
      -webkit-backdrop-filter: var(--blur-glass);
      backdrop-filter: var(--blur-glass);
    }
  }

  /*
   * OPEN STATE HAS EXACTLY ONE OWNER, AND IT IS JAVASCRIPT.
   *
   * This previously read `:hover, :focus-within, .force-open`, and the panel
   * could therefore never be closed. Clicking the trigger FOCUSES it, which
   * satisfies :focus-within, so the panel stayed open while the JS removed
   * .force-open and set aria-expanded="false". The control reported a state it
   * did not have (WCAG 4.1.2) and Escape failed identically, because its
   * handler ends by returning focus to the trigger.
   *
   * Three independent sources of truth for one boolean is the defect. Now the
   * script owns it via data-open, and hover is implemented in the script too,
   * so an explicit close always wins over a pointer that happens to be resting
   * over the menu.
   *
   * The :not([data-js]) pair is the no-JS fallback and nothing else. The script
   * stamps data-js on init, which retires it. Without it, a reader with no
   * JavaScript would find these links unreachable from the desktop nav.
   */
  .ca-dropdown:not([data-js]):hover .ca-mega,
  .ca-dropdown:not([data-js]):focus-within .ca-mega,
  .ca-dropdown[data-open='true'] .ca-mega {
    display: grid;
  }

  .ca-mega-col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.35rem;
  }
  /* ── THE RECIPE IS GONE FROM HERE. A-61, 2026-08-04 ──────────────────────
   *
   * This block declared the display face, --t-micro, weight 800, 0.08em and
   * uppercase, and Footer.astro declared the same five properties byte for byte
   * for its column titles. Both now carry `grouplabel` from styles/labels.css,
   * which is the one place that recipe is written; check-treatments.js rule 10
   * fails the build if any file under src/ but that one declares it again.
   *
   * WHAT IS LEFT IS WHAT IS NOT THE RECIPE. The muted ink is this label's own
   * argument — inside a floating panel it is a divider between columns rather
   * than a heading in the page, which is why it is quieter than the footer's —
   * and the 0.35rem is the gap to the first link, which belongs to this stack. */
  .ca-mega-label {
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }
  .ca-mega-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 0.65rem;
    padding: 0.55rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    text-decoration: none;
    /* 24px is the WCAG 2.2 target-size minimum; the icon and two lines of text
       carry it well past that, but a one-line row would not, so it is stated
       rather than left to the content. */
    min-block-size: 24px;
  }
  .ca-mega-item:hover,
  .ca-mega-item:focus-visible {
    background: var(--c-card-hover);
  }
  .ca-mega-item strong {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    font-weight: 700;
    /* Inherits the row's colour, which the nav ink block in Nav.astro sets and
       lifts on hover. It used to hardcode --c-text here, which is why the row
       looked identical hovered and unhovered: it was already at full white and
       had nowhere to go. */
  }
  .ca-mega-desc {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    margin-top: 0.15rem;
  }
  .ca-mega-icon {
    flex: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: 0.15rem;
  }
</style>
